::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q01::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which action best shows active listening in a newsroom discussion? {
~Interrupting to explain why your idea is better#Incorrect. Interrupting prevents careful listening and can weaken collaboration.
=Restating a teammate's concern before offering a solution#Correct. Restating shows that you heard the concern and are responding to it.
~Ignoring the concern so the team can move faster#Incorrect. Ignoring concerns can cause accuracy and teamwork problems.
~Changing the topic whenever disagreement appears#Incorrect. Avoiding the issue does not solve the newsroom problem.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q02::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
What is the most ethical response when a team wants to publish before checking a questionable fact? {
~Publish immediately because deadlines are always more important than accuracy.#Incorrect. Deadlines matter, but accuracy cannot be ignored.
~Delete the entire story without discussion.#Incorrect. This skips collaboration and may be unnecessary.
=Assign a teammate to verify the fact while others continue final edits.#Correct. This protects accuracy and uses teamwork efficiently.
~Replace the fact with a guess that sounds believable.#Incorrect. Guessing damages trust and accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q03::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Which workflow step best supports time management? {
~Waiting until the final minute to decide roles#Incorrect. Late role assignment can cause missed tasks.
~Letting every teammate work on the same task without a plan#Incorrect. This can waste time and create confusion.
~Skipping final review to save time#Incorrect. Skipping review can allow errors into publication.
=Setting role deadlines for reporting, editing, fact checking, and final review#Correct. Clear checkpoints help the team finish quality work on time.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q04::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which compromise protects publication quality? {
=Revise the headline so it stays interesting but matches the verified facts.#Correct. This respects audience interest while protecting accuracy.
~Keep the unsupported headline because it sounds exciting.#Incorrect. Excitement does not excuse inaccurate or unsupported claims.
~Let the loudest teammate decide without discussion.#Incorrect. Ethical collaboration requires respectful discussion.
~Remove the editor from the process to avoid disagreement.#Incorrect. Avoiding disagreement does not build cohesive work.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q05::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Why does collaboration matter in journalism? {
~It allows one person to control every decision.#Incorrect. Collaboration depends on shared responsibility, not control by one person.
=It helps a team combine roles, accuracy checks, and respectful decisions into cohesive work.#Correct. Strong collaboration improves the final publication.
~It makes deadlines unnecessary.#Incorrect. Collaborative teams still need deadlines.
~It replaces the need for ethical choices.#Incorrect. Collaboration requires ethical choices.
}
